 ## Overview
-The Material-Form Differential Path Integral is a method for computing the gradient of a radiometric measurement with respect to scene parameters $\pi$ by transforming the integration domain from a deforming spatial path space to a fixed "material space" [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem that standard differentiation of path integrals fails when the integration domain or the integrand's discontinuities depend on the parameters being differentiated [S1][S3, unofficial]. Unlike the Spatial-Form approach, which integrates over the moving spatial domain and requires handling all parameter-dependent discontinuities (including static mesh edges), the material form maps the domain to a reference geometry, reducing the boundary term to only include visibility-related discontinuities (silhouettes) [S2][S3, unofficial]. It builds on [Differentiable volume rendering](43-differentiable-volume-rendering.md) by extending differentiable path tracing to subsurface scattering [S1] and implicit surfaces [S2] where domain deformation is significant.
+The Material-Form Differential Path Integral is a method for computing the gradient of a radiometric measurement with respect to scene parameters $\pi$ by transforming the integration domain from a deforming spatial path space to a fixed "material space" [S1][S2][S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem that standard differentiation of path integrals fails when the integration domain or the integrand's discontinuities depend on the parameters being differentiated [S1][S3, unofficial]. Unlike the Spatial-Form approach, which integrates over the moving spatial domain and requires handling all parameter-dependent discontinuities (including static mesh edges), the material form maps the domain to a reference geometry, reducing the boundary term to only include visibility-related discontinuities (silhouettes) [S2][S3, unofficial]. It builds on [Differentiable volume rendering](43-differentiable-volume-rendering.md) by extending differentiable path tracing to subsurface scattering and implicit surfaces where domain deformation is significant [S1][S2].
 
